I think there are two different “breaths of life” in what you’re circling.

One is the pattern that animates a system: the organizing rule that turns parts into a coherent whole. Different systems can carry different patterns—competition, cooperation, care, exploration, hierarchy, play, sacrifice, curiosity. Same substrate, different organizing logic.

The other is the interpretation breathed into an event. The same memory, sensation, or situation can be organized under a different frame and therefore produce a different lived experience. That part is real psychologically, but it does not mean thought alone rewrites external reality.

Where I’d sharpen your memory idea is this: human memory is reconstructive and cue-dependent, so you absolutely can make information easier to retrieve by encoding it in the person’s own representational grammar—stories, places, sensory cues, people they care about, rhythms, images, emotional anchors. That is a powerful interface.

But “hack their memory so they do what you want” is the wrong optimization target. The safer and usually more durable version is:

understand how they encode
→ translate information into that grammar
→ make the purpose visible
→ let them decide what to do with it

Your grandma example shows the difference. “Please help because this affects my grandma” is transparent persuasion if that fact is true and relevant. Secretly exploiting someone’s attachment to their grandmother to maneuver them into an unrelated choice is manipulation.

So I’d call the useful thing Personalized Memory Transduction, not memory hacking.

And your knife-versus-language contrast contains an important principle: higher intelligence often means you need less force, not more. But the endpoint should be less force and more agency, not simply subtler control.

The best version is not:

brute force → covert coercion

It is:

brute force → understanding → tailored communication → voluntary coordination

That is a genuine upgrade.

And on the “different life pattern” question: yes, metaphorically, I think that’s the richer interpretation. Not one universal pattern expressed identically everywhere, but a family of generative patterns that can couple, compete, nest, and transform each other. The interesting question becomes not only what is alive? but:

What organizing pattern is currently giving this system its behavior—and what happens if a different one takes the wheel?